[center][size=6][b]Waypoints[/b][/size]
[size=3]Set a waypoint on the map and follow the guide to it, like in most open world games.[/size][/center]

[line]

[size=5][b]Features[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Set waypoints on the map screen[/b] - with a controller (at the crosshair) or a mouse (at the cursor). Hover a dungeon, minecart station, soulstorm or any other map marker and the waypoint snaps exactly onto it. Press again on the waypoint to remove it.
[*][b]Follow the guide, like a quest[/b] - press the guidance button (left stick click) and the game's own guidance line shows the way along a walkable route. The route goes around lakes, cliffs and walls, and keeps updating as you go.
[*][b]On the map and the minimap[/b] - your waypoint and the route are drawn on both. When the waypoint is beyond the minimap, an arrow on its edge points the way.
[*][b]Distance and direction[/b] - a marker over the waypoint with its distance, and an arrow at the edge of the screen when it's off screen.
[*][b]Overworld and the Sift[/b] - set a waypoint on either dimension's map. You're guided only while you're in its dimension, and it's kept for when you go back.
[*][b]Waypoint reached[/b] - a banner in the game's own area banner style when you arrive.
[*][b]Soulstorm generators[/b] - during a soulstorm, its generators show on screen (with their distance), on the minimap and on the map, where you can snap a waypoint to them.
[*][b]Fits in[/b] - a "Set Waypoint" hint with the game's own button icon on the map screen, and settings in the game's Mods tab.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Controls[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]On the map screen:[/b] left stick click (controller) or F6 (keyboard) sets the waypoint at the crosshair or cursor, or removes it if it's there.
[*][b]In the world:[/b] left stick click or G shows the guide line. F6 sets a waypoint under the mouse (on the ground or the minimap). F7 clears the waypoint.
[*][b]Pinging[/b] also sets the waypoint (can be turned off).
[/list]

All keys can be changed in the game's Settings, Mods tab.

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/minecraftdungeons2/mods/2]Blueprint Loader[/url] (2.0 or newer, for the settings page)
[/list]

[size=5][b]Install[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Install Blueprint Loader.
[*]Extract the [b]Waypoints[/b] folder from the zip into the game's mods folder, so that you have [i]~mods\Waypoints\Waypoints_P.pak[/i]:
Steam: [i]...\steamapps\common\Minecraft Dungeons II\Dungeons\Content\Paks\~mods[/i]
Xbox app: [i]...\Minecraft Dungeons II\Content\Dungeons\Content\Paks\~mods[/i]
[*]Start the game.
[/list]

To uninstall, delete the Waypoints folder.

[size=5][b]Settings[/b][/size] (Settings, Mods tab, Waypoints)

[list]
[*]Keys for setting, clearing and showing the path
[*]Pinging also sets the waypoint
[*]Path style: the game's guidance line on the guidance button (like quests), or dots that are always shown
[*]Whether the quest's own line also shows while a waypoint is set
[*]The path on the minimap, the distance and off-screen arrow, the path's length and colour
[*]Soulstorm generators on or off
[*]Update notice (on by default): tells you when a new version is out. It asks GitHub for the latest release when the game starts, at most every 6 hours; nothing else is sent.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Good to know[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]The minimap part appears after a few seconds of walking in a couple of directions, the first time: the mod learns how the minimap is drawn, then remembers it.
[*]Soulstorm generators show once you're within about 40 m of them: that's when the game creates them. Ones you've seen stay marked until destroyed.
[*]Walkable routes are found as far as the game has the area loaded (about 120 m); beyond that the route heads straight for the waypoint and keeps turning into a real path as you go.
[*]The mod runs on your game only. In co-op it works for you; other players' pings don't set your waypoint.
[*]Made for game version 1.1.2. A game update may need an updated version of the mod.
[/list]

